[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game"]
[Variation "Richter-Veresov Attack"]
[ECO "D01"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "break-the-pin,central-pawn-break"]
[For "black"]
[Kind "rare"]
[Opponent "2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bg5"]
[Start "1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6"]

3. Bg5 Nbd7 { Prepares for Bxf6: Black recaptures with a developed knight instead of the pawn, so the structure stays healthy. } 4. Nf3 ( 4. f3 c6 5. e4 dxe4 6. fxe4 e5 { Hits the centre at once; White's pieces are not ready to answer. } 7. Nf3 Qa5 8. Qd2 exd4 9. Nxd4 Bb4 10. Bd3 Ne5 ) 4... e6 { Frees the f8 bishop and holds d5. The bishop on g5 has not got anything to attack. } 5. e3 Be7 { Develops with a threat to trade off White's bishop if it stays on g5. } 6. Bd3 c5 { Hits d4 now that White's bishop has used a tempo. Black gets the same centre break as in the main lines. } 7. O-O O-O { Castles with equality; the extra moves White used on the bishop show nothing in the centre. Black has a fully ordinary game. } 8. a4 b6 { Plan: ...Bb7 and ...Rc8, then trade on d4 when it helps. If White plays Ne5, trade it with ...Nxe5 and answer dxe5 with ...Nd7 attacking the pawn. } *
